        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.085,
            0.899,
            0.135
          ],
          "content": "**Figure 11.7 shows a combination of resistors in which three resistors are connected together between points X and Y. Here, the resistors are said to be connected in parallel.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.381,
            0.16,
            0.796,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.455,
                0.375,
                0.724,
                0.39
              ],
              "content": "Figure 11.7 Resistors in parallel"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.277,
            0.42,
            0.553,
            0.439
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "11.6.1 Resistors in Series"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.448,
            0.901,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": "**What happens to the value of current when a number of resistors are connected in series in a circuit? What would be their equivalent resistance? Let us try to understand these with the help of the following activities.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.542,
            0.484,
            0.566
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 11.4"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.322,
            0.586,
            0.876,
            0.656
          ],
          "content": "**Join three resistors of different values in series. Connect them with a battery, an ammeter and a plug key, as shown in Fig. 11.6. You may use the resistors of values like 1** Ω**, 2** Ω**, 3** Ω **etc., and a battery of 6 V for performing this Activity.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.302,
            0.661,
            0.671,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": "**Plug the key. Note the ammeter reading.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.303,
            0.682,
            0.875,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "**Change the position of ammeter to anywhere in between the resistors. Note the ammeter reading each time.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.302,
            0.722,
            0.874,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": "**Do you find any change in the value of current through the ammeter?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.787,
            0.901,
            0.853
          ],
          "content": "**You will observe that the value of the current in the ammeter is the same, independent of its position in the electric circuit. It means that in a series combination of resistors the current is the same in every part of the circuit or the same current through each resistor.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.09,
